    <comment>New package: dsdcc 1.9.6, GPL-3.0-or-later.

DSDcc is a C++ digital speech decoder library for the DMR, dPMR, D-Star
and Yaesu System Fusion (YSF) digital voice standards. It is a rewrite of
the original DSD project as a real library: callers push 48 kS/s samples
into a decoder object instead of the decoder pulling from a file or
device. The submission ships libdsdcc1 (SONAME libdsdcc.so.1), dsdcc-devel
with the headers and libdsdcc.pc, and the dsdccx stdin/stdout decoder in
the main package.

Why it belongs in Factory: it is a build dependency of SDRangel, which
today exists only in hardware:sdr and whose own Factory submission is a
separate effort that has not landed and may not. Upstream's README states
the library was written specifically to be integrated as an SDRangel
plugin. dsdcc is nonetheless useful on its own: dsdccx decodes these
standards as a shell filter, and Factory currently carries no other
decoder for them.

PLEASE RULE ON ONE LICENCE POINT (flagged deliberately, not hidden):

descramble.cpp carries two notices. F4EXB's own header at the top places
the file under GPL-3, but below it sits an older notice for Jonathan
Naylor G4KLX's pcrepeatercontroller code reading &quot;version 2 of the
License.&quot; with no later-version clause - i.e. GPL-2.0-only, which does
not upgrade to GPL-3. The file is compiled into libdsdcc, and
descramble.h ships in -devel. F4EXB appears to have relicensed his
derivative, but whether that disposes of the G4KLX grant is a legal call,
not a packaging one, so I am surfacing it rather than deciding it. I
audited all 64 sources; descramble.cpp is the only GPL-2.0-only file.

Remaining licence findings, recorded in a Legal-Review-Notice above the
License tag so they need not be re-derived:

- Upstream ships no LICENSE file at all.
- 58 of 64 sources carry a box header truncated after &quot;as version 3 of
  the License, or&quot; - the later-version clause is simply absent. That
  wording is identical in the v1.0.0 tag from 2016, so it is upstream's
  own text, not a formatting accident. Read as or-later because the same
  author's locator.{cpp,h} carry the clause in full and his in-tree
  debian/copyright declares GPL-3.0+. Debian reached the same conclusion.
- nxdnconvolution.{cpp,h} and nxdncrc.{cpp,h} are GPL-2.0-or-later
  (G4KLX), which GPL-3.0-or-later absorbs cleanly.

Packaging notes:

- Maintained in hardware:sdr since 2017. 1.9.6 (2025-09-20) is current
  upstream; Debian and NixOS ship the same version.
- mbelib is disabled (-DUSE_MBELIB=OFF). It implements the patent-
  encumbered AMBE/IMBE vocoder, is not in Factory, and upstream itself
  documents building without it as the way to avoid that exposure.
  Without mbelib the library extracts raw MBE frames only.
- Upstream hardcodes CMAKE_INSTALL_RPATH to &lt;prefix&gt;/lib, and the %cmake
  macro only strips it on suse_version &lt;= 1500, so both shipped ELF
  objects carried a bogus lib64-less RUNPATH. Fixed with
  -DCMAKE_SKIP_INSTALL_RPATH:BOOL=ON; readelf now shows none.
- No %check. Upstream's testfec/ programs only print their results and
  return 0 unconditionally, so wiring them up would be a check that can
  never fail. They are not referenced by CMakeLists.txt at all.
- The tracked tarball was named .tar.xz while actually being gzip; it has
  been renamed to .tar.gz. Content is unchanged and byte-identical to the
  upstream v1.9.6 archive.</comment>
